## Claim
Text: "White House coronavirus adviser Dr. Anthony Fauci wrote a 2008 paper that found most 1918-1919 Spanish Influenza pandemic deaths were because of bacterial pneumonia from wearing masks."
Claim author: Dawn Hellewell
Claim date: October 19, 2020
Claim origin: https://web.archive.org/web/20201020155800/https://www.facebook.com/photo.php?fbid=10157592306850779&set=a.10154146794715779&type=3

## Initial Q&A
### Did Dr. Anthony Fauci co-author or author a paper published in 2008 that specifically examined the 1918–1919 Spanish Influenza pandemic?
Answer: Yes, Dr. Anthony Fauci co-authored a 2008 paper published in the *Journal of Infectious Diseases* that specifically examined the 1918–1919 Spanish Influenza pandemic, with the study finding that "the vast majority of influenza deaths resulted from secondary bacterial pneumonia."

Source URL: https://www.cidrap.umn.edu/influenza-vaccines/study-bacterial-pneumonia-was-main-killer-1918-flu-pandemic

### Does the 2008 paper by Dr. Anthony Fauci state that the majority of deaths in the 1918–1919 Spanish Influenza pandemic were caused by bacterial pneumonia?
Answer: Yes, the 2008 paper co-authored by Dr. Anthony Fauci states that the majority of deaths in the 1918–1919 Spanish Influenza pandemic were caused by secondary bacterial pneumonia. The report explicitly says that "the vast majority of influenza deaths resulted from secondary bacterial pneumonia" and that "more than 90% actually died of invading bacterial pneumonia" after the virus damaged the lungs. However, the paper does **not** claim that wearing masks caused these deaths; that assertion is not found in the study and is a misrepresentation of its findings.

Source URL: https://www.cidrap.umn.edu/influenza-vaccines/study-bacterial-pneumonia-was-main-killer-1918-flu-pandemic

### Does the 2008 paper by Dr. Anthony Fauci claim that wearing masks during the 1918–1919 pandemic led to bacterial pneumonia and subsequent deaths?
Answer: The 2008 paper by Dr. Anthony Fauci and colleagues does not mention masks or claim that wearing masks caused bacterial pneumonia or deaths during the 1918–1919 pandemic. Instead, it concludes that the majority of deaths likely resulted from secondary bacterial pneumonia following influenza virus infection, with no reference to mask use as a contributing factor.

Source URL: https://academic.oup.com/jid/article/198/7/962/2192118

### What specific evidence or data does the 2008 paper provide to support a causal link between mask-wearing and bacterial pneumonia deaths?
Answer: The 2008 paper does not provide any evidence or data supporting a causal link between mask-wearing and bacterial pneumonia deaths; in fact, the paper does not mention masks or mask-wearing anywhere in its text. Instead, it concludes that the majority of deaths in the 1918–1919 influenza pandemic resulted from secondary bacterial pneumonia following viral infection, based on histological examination of lung tissue and review of autopsy series.

Source URL: https://academic.oup.com/jid/article/198/7/962/2192118

### What was the actual primary cause of death identified in the 2008 paper for the 1918–1919 pandemic, and how does it compare to the claim?
Answer: The 2008 paper (and the broader consensus reported in the New Scientist article) identified bacterial pneumonia following influenza infection as the primary cause of death in the 1918–1919 pandemic, not the wearing of masks. The claim falsely asserts that the paper linked deaths to mask use; in reality, the research concluded that “bacterial pneumonia that came on the heels of mostly mild cases of flu killed the majority” of victims, and Anthony Fauci himself agreed that “bacterial pneumonia played a major role.” Thus, the claim misrepresents both the cause and the paper’s content.

Source URL: https://www.newscientist.com/article/dn14458-bacteria-were-the-real-killers-in-1918-flu-pandemic/

### Are there any other peer-reviewed studies or historical records that confirm that mask-wearing during the 1918 pandemic caused bacterial pneumonia?
Answer: The search result does not contain any mention of mask-wearing as a cause of bacterial pneumonia during the 1918 pandemic. The NIH article and the 2008 study by Fauci et al. describe secondary bacterial pneumonia following viral damage as the primary cause of death, but they do not attribute the bacterial pneumonia to mask use. Therefore, this source does not provide evidence to confirm that claim.

Source URL: https://www.nih.gov/news-events/news-releases/bacterial-pneumonia-caused-most-deaths-1918-influenza-pandemic

### What was the context of the 2008 paper—was it a review, an original research article, or an opinion piece, and did it make any recommendations about mask use?
Answer: The 2008 paper by Morens, Taubenberger, and Fauci is an original research article—it presents new histological analysis of 58 autopsy specimens and a review of 109 published autopsy series—not a review or opinion piece. It concludes that most deaths in the 1918 influenza pandemic resulted from secondary bacterial pneumonia, and it recommends that pandemic planning prioritize prevention, diagnosis, prophylaxis, and treatment of bacterial pneumonia, as well as stockpiling antibiotics and bacterial vaccines. **The paper makes no mention of mask use or any recommendation about masks.**

Source URL: https://academic.oup.com/jid/article/198/7/962/2192118

## Final Judgement
The key insight from the fact-check is that Dr. Anthony Fauci co-authored a 2008 paper that indeed found the majority of deaths in the 1918–1919 Spanish Influenza pandemic were due to secondary bacterial pneumonia. However, the paper never mentions masks or attributes any deaths to mask-wearing. Multiple sources confirm that the paper's conclusion is about bacterial pneumonia following viral lung damage, with no causal link to masks. The claim falsely asserts that the paper found deaths were "because of bacterial pneumonia from wearing masks," which is a clear misrepresentation of the paper's content.

The evidence is clear and no further investigation is required.

`refuted`

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The 2008 paper co-authored by Dr. Anthony Fauci [found that the vast majority of 1918–1919 influenza deaths resulted from secondary bacterial pneumonia](https://www.cidrap.umn.edu/influenza-vaccines/study-bacterial-pneumonia-was-main-killer-1918-flu-pandemic), but the paper [makes no mention of masks or any causal link between mask-wearing and those deaths](https://academic.oup.com/jid/article/198/7/962/2192118). The claim falsely asserts that the paper attributed deaths to "bacterial pneumonia from wearing masks," which is a misrepresentation; the paper instead attributes the pneumonia to viral lung damage and recommends pandemic planning focus on preventing and treating secondary bacterial infections, not on mask use.